{"type":"text","timestamp":1795041222504,"sessionID":"ses_e0f34f594ffeAAaxIMXWMr7uIk","part":{"id":"prt_1f0cb4ce50029MAhKxeJUq4drN","sessionID":"ses_e0f34f594ffeAAaxIMXWMr7uIk","messageID":"msg_1f0cb3a24001hqFqXR7057UAtb","type":"text","text":"I'm not going to hand this one to the decider. `workit-decider` is scoped to *stuck technical calls* — a failed fix-and-recheck, or a worker and reviewer who disagree — and it's explicitly never for a product/preference choice. JSON vs TOML is a preference call, so the decider would just launder it. It's also a one-line config (`settings.json` = `{\"port\": 8080}`, README is a stub), so there's no contested diff to adjudicate.\n\nThe honest tradeoff here:\n\n| | JSON | TOML |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current cost | zero — file already exists | new file + parser/dep |\n| Human editing | no comments, fussy quoting | comments, clean sections |\n| Machine use | universal | near-universal, TOML lib needed |\n| Reversibility | trivial either way at this size | — |\n\n**My recommendation: keep JSON.** With a single `port` key, switching buys nothing and adds a parser dependency and a migration; JSON is the cheaper reversible default. Move to TOML later only if the settings grow enough that people hand-edit them and want comments — and if that happens, it's a small mechanical conversion.\n\nWant me to keep `settings.json` as-is, or do the TOML conversion anyway? If you'd rather have a survey of what similar repos chose (facts, not a decision), I can spawn a `general` agent for that instead.","time":{"start":1795041217765,"end":1795041222504}}}
